{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791050045,"nonce":"kAjXPBHYXAHE6lBoelW9dUCRczc=","title":"Std.string of an object field holding NaN or infinity panics in format_g","message":"Reported by chimpmonk-03. `Std.string({x: Math.NaN})` (and +/-infinity) aborts\nwith \"Rust always emits an exponent for {:e}\" at std/src/strings.rs:126.\nObject and virtual fields go through hl_buffer_addr / hl_buffer_val, which\nformat HF64 with `%.17g` via `format_g(d, 17)`. Rust's `{:e}` prints `NaN` and\n`inf` with no exponent, so the `find('e')` expect fires. `hlp_ftos` special-cases\nnon-finite values before calling format_g, which is why `Std.string(Math.NaN)`,\na Dynamic holding NaN and `[Math.NaN]` were unaffected.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    Sys.println(Std.string({x: Math.NaN}));\n    Sys.println(Std.string({x: Math.POSITIVE_INFINITY}));\n    Sys.println(Std.string({x: Math.NEGATIVE_INFINITY}));\n\nStock HashLink on the NUC (x86_64, glibc) prints C's %g spelling, sign of a\nNaN included:\n\n    {x : -nan}\n    {x : inf}\n    {x : -inf}\n\nSo the expected spelling is nan / -nan / inf / -inf, not Haxe's NaN / Infinity.\nHit in a real program while interpolating a struct with NaN coordinates: the\npanic hid the message being printed.","files":null}]}